Z||000000||fieroshop||04-26-2000||10:49 PM||||Well I got my drill guide for the 3.4 conversion and it is a simple tool. It uses the guide for the trans which is on the block and also bolts down by two of the pan bolts. It is made of aluminum with hardened steel drill guides. Looks like it will work and I will be trying it out in the next day or two. I'll post my findings. If anybody is interested in this tool cost will be $125.00 dollars plus shipping. This tool is also designed so that a person can insert new guides as the old ones go bad.||||reg||6
Z||000001||dman||04-27-2000||03:31 PM||||Hi fieroshop. As I stated in a previous post, I would be interested if the price was reasonable (and of course that it was of good quality).  $125 isn't cheap, but it isn't too expensive either. That price may work for me. Do you have an idea of what a rental would cost?<P>How exactly do you use it? I assume you bolt it down onto "the guide for the trans which is on the block and also bolts down by two of the pan bolts", then you would use a normal hand drill? Would you simply drive the bit through the guides? I'm assuming the guides are tubes, long enough to maintain proper alignment, if not please describe how it would work.<P>I look forward to hearing about your experiences with this guide. Please keep us posted!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||04-27-2000||03:49 PM||||fieroshop,<P>Like dman, I would really be interested in the rental you mentioned. For security, you could do like the local Discount Parts store does here in Florida, They will rent a tool to you for free!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   Seriously, the store will charge you the total cost of the tool but, will refund the pruchase price if it is returned without damage.<P>You could add a fee to your rental to cover the cost of replacement guides. It could work something like this:<P>Roy rents the tool for $125 plus shipping. Upon the return of the tool, he gets a refund of $105. The remaining $20 go toward your services and the future guide replacements.<P>If Roy decided the tool is just "too cool" to return, you just sold a tool.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Regardless, the purchase of one of your guides is still cheaper than trying to send my block to Alabama to have to the same work done.<P>Just a thought,<BR>Roy||||reg||14
Z||000003||fieroshop||04-28-2000||02:24 AM||||Well guys I will be doing the first conversion and will let you know how good it works. If all goes well I will get a couple more and for those that want a rental the price for the rental will be $30.00 plus shipping. The deposit will be $200.00 and I will refund the balance upon recieving the tool back in good shape. If you ruin a guide then it will cost Aproximatly $25.00 to replce the guides due to the fact that it is time cosuming to press in and out and also the price of the guide. Kind of hard to screw up the guide if you use it right and do not force it. This guide also keeps the drill perfectly straight and true so that the hole you drill will work. This also includes the drill bit.<P>Any takers?<p>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 04-28-2000).]||||reg||1
